# ISO vs APT delivery warnings

After finishing a change set (or when summarizing work), classify how it
ships and **warn the user explicitly**. Do not assume they will rebuild or
upgrade unless they say so.

## APT only → publish testing + upgrade

Touches under `packages/` (e.g. `fosos-desktop`, `fosos-cli`, `fosos-theme`,
`fosos-installer`, `fosos-welcome`, `fosos-archive-keyring`) and nothing in
the ISO build tree.

Warn with wording like:

> **APT:** publish these packages to **testing** (Actions → APT publish, or
> `sudo bash scripts/apt-archive.sh publish`), then on the machine run
> `sudo fos update && sudo fos upgrade` (or `apt update && apt upgrade`).

Name the packages that changed when known.

## New ISO required

Touches live image / builder inputs, for example:

- `build/live-build/config/` (package lists, hooks, includes, bootloaders)
- `scripts/build-iso.sh`, `scripts/check-build-config.sh`, `scripts/stage-deb.sh`
- Greeter/live-only assets that only land via image rebuild
- Firmware / package-list changes meant for the live or installed image seed

Warn with wording like:

> **ISO:** this needs a **new ISO** (`sudo bash scripts/build-iso.sh` per
> `docs/BUILDING.md`). An apt upgrade on an existing install is not enough
> for this change.

## Both

If both trees changed, give **both** warnings. Note that `packages/` also
ships inside the ISO via staged `.deb`s, so a full install-media test still
needs a rebuild even when apt covers upgrades on existing systems.

## Neither

Docs-only, `.cursor/`, or repo meta with no package/ISO impact: no delivery
warning.

## Do not

- Run publish, promote, or ISO build unless the user asks
- Promote testing → stable unless the user asks
- Skip the warning because the change “looks small”
